---
import LearnArticle from "../../components/LearnArticle.astro";

const breaks = [
	{ problem: "You can only squeeze lemons so fast", fix: "Hire helpers and open more stands" },
	{ problem: "Every stand makes the lemonade a bit differently", fix: "Write the recipe down so every stand follows the same rules" },
	{ problem: "Customers line up at one stand while another sits empty", fix: "Send each customer to whichever stand has the shortest line" },
	{ problem: "You run out of lemons by noon", fix: "Find a supplier who delivers to every stand" },
	{ problem: "If you get sick, the whole business stops", fix: "Make sure no single person is the only one who can do a job" },
];
---

<LearnArticle
	href="/learn/scalability"
	description="Scalability explained with a lemonade stand that becomes a chain: what breaks as demand grows, scaling up vs scaling out, and why the internet was designed to grow."
	lead="A lemonade stand works fine for twenty customers. What happens when two thousand show up?"
>
	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">The Lemonade Stand</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			You set up a table, a jug, and a stack of cups. On a normal afternoon you sell about twenty
			cups — easy. Then a video of your lemonade goes viral, and the next day there's a line down the
			street.
		</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			Suddenly everything that worked before starts to go wrong. Your arms get tired. The jug is
			always empty. People give up and leave.
		</p>
		<p class="block-callout">
			A system is <em>scalable</em> if it can keep working well as it grows — much more work, many more
			users — without falling apart or having to be rebuilt from scratch.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">Two Ways to Grow</p>
		<div class="compare-grid">
			<div class="compare-card">
				<p class="compare-card-title">Scale up: get bigger</p>
				<p>Buy a giant jug and an electric juicer. One stand, but more powerful.</p>
				<p>Simple — but there's a limit. There's only so big a jug can get.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="compare-card compare-card--accent">
				<p class="compare-card-title">Scale out: add more</p>
				<p>Open more stands around town, each with its own helper.</p>
				<p>Can keep growing — but only if the stands work together well.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
		<p class="block-text">
			Computers grow in the same two ways. A website can move to one bigger, faster computer (scaling
			up), or it can spread its work across many ordinary computers working side by side (scaling
			out). The biggest websites in the world scale out — they run on thousands of computers at once.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">What Has to Change</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			Turning one stand into a chain means solving problems you never had before:
		</p>
		<div class="table-wrap">
			<table class="data-table">
				<thead>
					<tr>
						<th>What breaks</th>
						<th>What has to change</th>
					</tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					{
						breaks.map((row) => (
							<tr>
								<td>{row.problem}</td>
								<td>{row.fix}</td>
							</tr>
						))
					}
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
		<p class="block-text">
			Every one of those has a computer version. Websites share the load across many computers, send
			each visitor to the least-busy one, follow shared rules so every computer gives the same answer,
			and keep spare copies so one breakdown doesn't take everything offline (more on that in
			<a href="/learn/redundancy-and-fault-tolerance">Redundancy &amp; Fault Tolerance</a>).
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">When Scaling Goes Wrong</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			You've probably seen a system that <em>didn't</em> scale: a ticket website that crashes the
			moment a big concert goes on sale, or a game's servers that fall over on launch day. Nothing was
			broken before — the system just wasn't built for that many people arriving at once.
		</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			Sometimes the limit is built right into the design. The original internet addresses had room
			for about 4.3 billion devices, which felt endless in 1981 — until the world ran out. That story
			is on the <a href="/learn/ipv4-vs-ipv6">IPv4 vs IPv6</a> page.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="block">
		<p class="block-title">The Internet Was Built to Scale</p>
		<p class="block-text">
			The internet itself is one of the most scalable systems ever built. It has grown from a handful
			of university computers to billions of devices, and it still works. A few design choices made
			that possible:
		</p>
		<ul class="block-list">
			<li>
				<em>No single boss computer.</em> There's no central machine everything has to go through, so
				there's no single bottleneck to overload.
			</li>
			<li>
				<em>Shared, open rules.</em> Anyone can connect a new network, as long as it follows the same
				agreed rules (called <em>protocols</em>) as everyone else.
			</li>
			<li>
				<em>Many possible routes.</em> Data can travel lots of different paths, so new connections add
				capacity instead of crowding one road — see <a href="/learn/packets-and-routing">Packets &amp; Routing</a>.
			</li>
		</ul>
	</section>
</LearnArticle>
